ask(c,'Which mapping matches the lecture’s method definitions?', ['Algebraic: identities/substitution; geometric: spatial relations/law of cosines; numerical: iteration','Algebraic: random trial only; geometric: text parsing; numerical: no computation','Algebraic: collision checking only; geometric: identical to all numerical iterations','Numerical: a closed expression requiring no iterations by definition'], ['These are the roles explicitly contrasted.','These descriptions do not match the definitions.','Collision checking is not the defining algebraic operation, and geometric analysis is distinct.','Numerical in the lecture refers to iterative computation.'],kind='match',pages=[278,280])
ask(c,'Which condition does the lecture present as sufficient for a6R closed-form IK solution?', ['Three neighboring joint axes intersect at a single point','Any three non-neighboring axes are merely nonparallel','All six axes must be distinct parallel lines','Every link length must be equal'], ['The source specifies neighboring axes and a common intersection.','Nonparallel and non-neighboring are weaker and different conditions.','Parallel lines are not the stated common-intersection condition.','Equal lengths are not the stated criterion.'],pages=[280])
ask(c,'What does “sufficient” mean for that geometric condition?', ['Meeting it supports the stated closed-form result, without saying it is necessary for every solvable design','Failing it proves no closed-form solution can ever exist','Meeting it makes every Cartesian pose reachable','Meeting it forces exactly one IK branch'], ['Sufficiency is one direction of implication, not necessity or uniqueness.','That would incorrectly treat the sufficient condition as necessary.','The condition concerns solving equations, not unlimited workspace.','Closed-form solutions can include multiple branches.'],pages=[280],difficulty='hard')
ask(c,'Which trade-off matches the lecture without treating speed claims as universal benchmarks?', ['Closed form exploits suitable geometry and can reduce repeated computation; numerical methods are more general but require safeguards','Numerical methods are always faster on every processor','Closed form removes all physical constraints','Numerical IK never uses a Jacobian'], ['The method comparison and summary emphasize geometry, computation and singularity handling.','No universal hardware benchmark is established.','Analytical solutions still obey reachability and admissibility.','The summary specifically names Jacobian-based numerical methods.'],pages=[280,293])
